\subsection{Overview}
\label{sec:overview}

Figure~\ref{fig:method} illustrates the architecture of \tool{}, our framework for learning policy-guided constraint simplification. \tool{} is designed as a closed-loop, pre-solving pipeline that iteratively simplifies a given \SMT{} formula. The framework's design follows a clear logical flow, where each component builds upon the last to enable intelligent, adaptive decision-making.

The process begins with a crucial preprocessing step variable normalization which creates a consistent foundation for learning. To ensure our models can generalize across formulas that are structurally identical but use different variable names, this stage canonicalizes the input by sorting variables based on a stable vector of their structural attributes, yielding a permutation-invariant representation for the agent.

This canonical formula is then transformed into a rich, semantic state by our Feature Encoding component. Using a pre-trained language model (PLM), it converts the symbolic text into a high-dimensional vector that captures the formula's underlying structure. This vector is fundamental to our approach, serving two critical functions. Primarily, it acts as the semantic state representation for our reinforcement learning agent, providing the rich, contextual input needed for its policy decisions. Additionally, we use these vectors offline to train two auxiliary predictors: a classifier that estimates a formula's solvability and another that predicts its solving time. As we will see, these pre-trained predictors are crucial for guiding the agent's learning.

At each step of the simplification loop, the feature encoder generates a real-time state vector that is provided to the dual-agent policy. This vector is simultaneously fed to the two pre-trained predictors, which provide immediate, estimated feedback on the potential impact of a simplification on both solvability and time. This feedback is essential for our hybrid reward system, which guides the agent's learning in an otherwise sparse-reward environment. The policy then acts: an \RL{} agent strategically selects \textit{what} to simplify (the variable), while an \LLM{} tactically proposes \textit{how} to simplify (the value). Each proposal is finally checked by the solver, providing the ground-truth outcome that closes the learning loop and drives policy updates.


