% To ensure efficient exploration and prevent revisiting futile search pathways, we introduce two key mechanisms. First, a sophisticated sequence-based counterexample management system is employed (Line~6). Instead of merely tracking failed variable-value pairs, our framework records entire sequences of assignments that lead to unsatisfiability. Before committing to a new assignment $(v_t, c_t)$, the system checks if the resulting path is a duplicate or a supersequence of a known failing trajectory, effectively pruning entire subtrees of the search space. Second, upon validating a novel assignment, the framework performs dynamic state re-encoding (Line~7). The \SMT{} formula is textually updated with the new concretization and then re-processed by the PLM encoder (as described in Section~\ref{sec:policy-formulation-and-optimization}) to generate a fresh embedding. This ensures that the RL agent's policy for the subsequent step is conditioned on the most current, simplified state of the problem, enabling more informed and contextually aware decisions. The backend SMT solver is then invoked on the simplified formula, guarded by a multi-level adaptive timeout (Line~8). This timeout is dynamically scheduled by our predictive guidance module (Section~\ref{sec:appendix-predictors}) to allocate resources judiciously, preventing excessive time on likely intractable paths. Finally, based on the solver's output, a multi-component reward is calculated to update the agent's policy (Lines~9--10), completing the loop. This cycle repeats until a solution is found or the global budget is exhausted.

